import{_ as t,o,c as d,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const f=JSON.parse('{"title":"The srt settings of the CLI frontends","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"components/sandbox.md","filePath":"components/sandbox.md"}'),a={name:"components/sandbox.md"};function n(i,e,c,l,r,h){return o(),d("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s(`<h1 id="the-srt-settings-of-the-cli-frontends" tabindex="-1">The srt settings of the CLI frontends <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-srt-settings-of-the-cli-frontends" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The srt settings of the CLI frontends&quot;">​</a></h1><p><code>srt</code>, the sandbox runtime, runs one process and everything that it starts inside one sandbox. It reads one settings file. <code>harness install</code> writes one such file for each CLI frontend that the profile&#39;s <code>[setup]</code> chooses:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/claude.json</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/opencode.json</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/omp.json</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/antigravity.json</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The launcher <code>agentbox</code> starts a frontend under <code>srt</code> with its file, as <a href="#the-launcher-agentbox">the launcher</a> describes. A frontend that starts without it does not read the file.</p><p>The files come from two sources. The <code>sandbox</code> section of the Claude Code settings template, <code>settings/settings.proposal.json</code>, stays the source of the lists that every frontend shares: the write paths, the write and read denies, the credential files and the network rules, with the domains that <code>settings/network.toml</code> and the profile&#39;s overlay give. The policy file <code>sandbox/policy.toml</code> holds only what <code>srt</code> adds to them. <code>lib/harness/sandbox.py</code> renders the files from both.</p><h2 id="the-policy-file" tabindex="-1">The policy file <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-policy-file" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The policy file&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>sandbox/policy.toml</code> has a table <code>[read]</code> and a table <code>[frontend.&lt;name&gt;]</code> for each of <code>claude</code>, <code>opencode</code>, <code>omp</code> and <code>antigravity</code>. Every path starts with <code>~/</code>.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>read.allow</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the paths that every frontend reads under the read limit</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.state</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the frontend&#39;s own directories and files, which it reads and writes</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.deny_write</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">write denies that the frontend adds to the template&#39;s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.own</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s deny entries that the frontend reads and writes itself: its own credential files, by name</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>frontend.&lt;name&gt;.read_files</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">files below a read deny directory that the frontend reads and does not write</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The read limit denies reads of the whole home directory and allows the paths of <code>read.allow</code> again. A path that the template denies stays hidden inside an allowed path: in <code>srt</code> a deny inside an allowed path wins. Each frontend&#39;s <code>own</code> lifts its own credential files from the deny lists of its file alone, and every other frontend&#39;s file still denies reads of them:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">frontend</th><th style="text-align:left;"><code>own</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>claude</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>~/.claude.json</code>, <code>~/.claude/.credentials.json</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>opencode</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>~/.local/share/opencode/auth.json</code>, <code>~/.local/share/opencode/mcp-auth.json</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>omp</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>~/.omp/agent/agent.db</code>, <code>~/.omp/agent/agent.db-wal</code>, <code>~/.omp/agent/.env</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>antigravity</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">none</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Claude Code writes <code>~/.claude.json</code> itself; a write deny of it would stop the whole process under <code>srt</code>. The cost is that a command that a frontend runs can also read and write the frontend&#39;s own files, because the sandbox of the whole process cannot tell the frontend from its own shell.</p><p>OpenCode&#39;s <code>read_files</code> are its three provider tokens, <code>gwdg-token</code>, <code>opendesign-token</code> and <code>tokenharbor-token</code> below <code>~/.config/api-keys</code>, which its configuration reads by file. A token has no deny entry of its own, so <code>own</code> cannot lift it; and <code>~/.config</code> is a write deny, so the token stays read-only. No other frontend has a <code>read_files</code> entry. oh-my-pi adds the write denies <code>~/.omp/agent/config.yml</code> and <code>~/.omp/agent/extensions</code>.</p><p><code>harness install</code> exits with status 2 on a policy file with an unknown key, a missing key or frontend, a value that is not a list of strings, a path that does not start with <code>~/</code>, or a path with a <code>..</code> segment, which could leave the home directory. The message names the key or the path. It also exits with status 2 on a <code>read_files</code> path that is itself an entry of the frontend&#39;s read denies, compared as paths after the <code>~</code> expansion, so <code>~/x/</code>, <code>~//x</code> and <code>~/./x</code> are <code>~/x</code>, and <code>~/</code> is the home directory; such a file goes in <code>own</code>.</p><h2 id="the-rendering-rules" tabindex="-1">The rendering rules <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rendering-rules" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rendering rules&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>srt_settings</code> gives one frontend&#39;s object, with the keys <code>network</code> and <code>filesystem</code> and no other:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">key</th><th style="text-align:left;">value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>network.allowedDomains</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, in order</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>network.deniedDomains</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, or <code>[]</code> where it has none; <code>srt</code> refuses a file without the key</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>network.allowLocalBinding</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.allowWrite</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, without each entry at or below another frontend&#39;s <code>state</code> path; then the frontend&#39;s <code>state</code>; then the real path of <code>$TMPDIR</code>, where it is set and not empty</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.denyWrite</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the template&#39;s, without the frontend&#39;s <code>own</code>; then its <code>deny_write</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.denyRead</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the home directory; then the template&#39;s; then each path of <code>credentials.files</code>; without the frontend&#39;s <code>own</code>; each literal entry other than the home directory that lies strictly above a path of the frontend&#39;s <code>read_files</code> written as the entry followed by <code>/*</code>, in its place</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>filesystem.allowRead</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>read.allow</code>; then the frontend&#39;s <code>state</code>; then its <code>own</code>; then its <code>read_files</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>Each <code>~</code> and <code>~/</code> prefix becomes the home directory, so the files hold absolute paths. A list holds no entry twice, and the first occurrence keeps its place. &quot;At or below&quot; compares whole path components: <code>~/.claude.json</code> is not below <code>~/.claude</code>. So no frontend&#39;s file allows a write to another frontend&#39;s state. A glob of the template, such as <code>~/Research/**/.git/hooks</code>, passes through after the expansion.</p><p>A file below a read deny directory is read by name, through a glob. <code>srt</code> emits a literal read deny that lies inside a read allow after the allows, so a file allow below it never takes effect; it emits a glob read deny without each file allow that the glob covers. So in OpenCode&#39;s file the literal <code>~/.config/api-keys</code> becomes <code>~/.config/api-keys/*</code>, and the three tokens join <code>allowRead</code>; every other frontend&#39;s file keeps the literal entry. The glob ends in <code>/*</code>, not <code>/**</code>: <code>srt</code> strips a trailing <code>/**</code> before it tells a glob from a literal, so <code>D/**</code> is the literal <code>D</code> again. <code>D/*</code> covers everything below <code>D</code> and not <code>D</code> itself, so OpenCode can list the names in <code>~/.config/api-keys</code> and read no other file there. An entry that holds <code>*</code>, <code>?</code>, <code>[</code> or <code>]</code> is a glob already and stays as it is, and the home directory is the read limit and is never converted. The <code>own</code> files join <code>allowRead</code> for the same reason: oh-my-pi&#39;s <code>~/.omp/agent/.env</code> lies below the template&#39;s glob <code>~/**/.env</code>, and only a file allow lifts it there.</p><p>The template&#39;s <code>excludedCommands</code> is not carried: under <code>srt</code> the whole process is inside the sandbox, so no command can run outside it. The masked credentials of <code>srt</code> are not used.</p><h2 id="the-install" tabindex="-1">The install <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-install" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The install&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>harness install</code> renders the Claude Code settings template as it does for <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>, with the profile and the network overlay, and gives its <code>sandbox</code> object to <code>srt_settings</code>. It writes each file as JSON indented by two spaces, with the keys in the order above and a final newline. The files belong to no frontend&#39;s plan: a change of them prints no restart line. A frontend that <code>[setup]</code> does not choose gets no file, and with no frontend chosen the install renders no file. The install does not change what it writes to <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code>. Because it renders the template with the network overlay for every chosen frontend, a broken overlay stops the install with status 2 also when <code>claude</code> is not chosen, for example an <code>allow</code> entry that is a URL.</p><p>With at least one frontend chosen, the install also copies the launcher <code>sandbox/agentbox</code> to <code>~/.local/bin/agentbox</code> with mode 755, a directory that a session cannot write. With <code>claude</code> chosen it writes Claude Code&#39;s override beside the settings files:</p><div class="language-text vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">text</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/claude-inner-off.json   {&quot;sandbox&quot;: {&quot;enabled&quot;: false}}</span></span></code></pre></div><p>A launcher whose mode differs from 755 is a change, as a file whose bytes differ is.</p><p>The install writes nothing through a symlink. So where <code>~/.local/bin</code> is a symlink and a frontend is chosen, <code>harness install --apply</code> exits with status 2, writes nothing, and names the path and the symlink.</p><h2 id="the-launcher-agentbox" tabindex="-1">The launcher agentbox <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-launcher-agentbox" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The launcher agentbox&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>agentbox</code> is a POSIX <code>sh</code> script that starts one CLI frontend under <code>srt</code>:</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">agentbox</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">fronten</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">d</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [args...]</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The frontends are the commands <code>claude</code>, <code>opencode</code>, <code>omp</code> and <code>agy</code>. <code>agentbox</code> runs</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">~</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">/.local/bin/srt --settings </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">~</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">/.config/research-harness/sandbox/</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">policy name</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">.json -- </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">\\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">    &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">program</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">&gt; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">extr</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">a</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> &lt;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">args..</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">.</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">&gt;</span></span></code></pre></div><p>with <code>exec</code>, so the exit status of <code>srt</code> is its own. The policy name is <code>antigravity</code> for <code>agy</code> and the frontend&#39;s name otherwise. <code>&lt;program&gt;</code> is the path that <code>command -v</code> gives on the caller&#39;s <code>PATH</code> without each entry that matches <code>*/cmux-cli-shims/*</code> or <code>*/cmux-cli-shims</code>. In a cmux terminal the first <code>claude</code> on the <code>PATH</code> is a shim of cmux, which <code>srt</code> cannot read; the shim itself skips the same entries to find the real <code>claude</code> where the cmux wrapper is absent. A <code>claude</code> under <code>agentbox</code> therefore runs without the cmux wrapper. The filter decides only which program runs: the program gets the caller&#39;s <code>PATH</code> unchanged. The caller&#39;s arguments follow <code>&lt;extra&gt;</code>, each passed as one argument, unchanged. The <code>--</code> ends the options of <code>srt</code>, which otherwise reads a <code>--settings</code> of the frontend as its own.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">frontend</th><th style="text-align:left;"><code>&lt;extra&gt;</code></th><th style="text-align:left;">why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>claude</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--settings ~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/claude-inner-off.json</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">Claude Code&#39;s own sandbox fails inside the sandbox of <code>srt</code>, so the override turns it off</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>opencode</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--standalone</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">OpenCode runs its server as its own child inside the sandbox, not in a background service that a run outside the launcher could have started</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>omp</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--approval-mode yolo</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">oh-my-pi runs its tools without a prompt; the sandbox of <code>srt</code> is the boundary</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>agy</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">nothing</td><td style="text-align:left;"></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The frontend gets <code>AGENTBOX=1</code> and the three cache variables below in its environment; every other variable stays the caller&#39;s. <code>agentbox</code> runs only the <code>srt</code> in <code>~/.local/bin</code>, never one found on the <code>PATH</code>. So it starts nothing before <code>harness setup</code> has installed <code>srt</code> in <code>~/.local/bin</code>.</p><p>Each run makes one new directory with <code>mktemp -d &quot;\${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/agentbox.XXXXXX&quot;</code>, below <code>$TMPDIR</code>, or below <code>/tmp</code> where <code>TMPDIR</code> is unset or empty, with the subdirectories <code>npm</code>, <code>uv</code> and <code>texmf-var</code>. The sandbox denies the caches of these tools in the home directory, so <code>agentbox</code> sets and exports a cache variable for each, where the caller&#39;s variable is unset or empty:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">variable</th><th style="text-align:left;">tool</th><th style="text-align:left;">value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>npm_config_cache</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">npm</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>&lt;run directory&gt;/npm</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>UV_CACHE_DIR</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">uv</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>&lt;run directory&gt;/uv</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>TEXMFVAR</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">LaTeX, for example the font cache of <code>lualatex</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>&lt;run directory&gt;/texmf-var</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>A caller&#39;s non-empty value stays. An empty value counts as unset: an empty <code>UV_CACHE_DIR</code> stops <code>uv</code>, and an empty <code>npm_config_cache</code> sends npm to <code>~/.npm</code>. The settings file of every frontend lets it write <code>/tmp</code> and the <code>$TMPDIR</code> of the install. Each run starts with empty caches, so npm and uv download again and <code>lualatex</code> builds its font cache again. The run directory stays after the run, because <code>agentbox</code> ends in <code>exec</code>; the system&#39;s cleaning of temporary directories removes it.</p><p><code>agentbox</code> exits with status 2, starts nothing and prints one line on stderr when:</p><ul><li>no frontend is named, or the name is not one of the four; the line lists the four;</li><li><code>AGENTBOX</code> is set, so it runs inside a launcher already;</li><li><code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> is not an executable file; the line names <code>harness setup</code>;</li><li>the frontend&#39;s settings file, or for <code>claude</code> the override, is not a file; the line names the file and <code>harness install --apply</code>;</li><li><code>command -v</code> finds no program of the frontend&#39;s name on the <code>PATH</code> without the cmux entries;</li><li>what <code>command -v</code> gives does not start with <code>/</code>: a shell function, for example a bash function that the caller exports and that macOS&#39;s <code>/bin/sh</code> imports, or, where <code>/bin/sh</code> is <code>dash</code>, a program in a relative <code>PATH</code> entry; the line names the program and what was found;</li><li>the frontend is <code>claude</code> and an argument is <code>--settings</code> or starts with <code>--settings=</code>: Claude Code keeps only the last <code>--settings</code>, so the caller&#39;s would replace the override and turn Claude Code&#39;s own sandbox on again. More settings go in the user settings instead;</li><li><code>mktemp</code> cannot make the run directory, for example because <code>$TMPDIR</code> is not a directory; the line names the directory.</li></ul><p>A refused run makes no run directory.</p><p><code>agentbox -h</code> and <code>agentbox --help</code> print the usage and exit 0.</p><h2 id="the-pinned-srt" tabindex="-1">The pinned srt <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-pinned-srt" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The pinned srt&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>harness setup</code> installs <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code>, the <code>srt</code> that <code>agentbox</code> runs, with any frontend chosen, from two files of the harness:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">file</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it holds</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>sandbox/srt/package.json</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">a private package with one dependency, <code>@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime</code> at exactly 0.0.81, and the override of <code>node-forge</code> to exactly 1.4.1</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>sandbox/srt/package-lock.json</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the tree that npm resolves from that file: srt 0.0.81, <code>node-forge</code> 1.4.1, <code>zod</code>, <code>commander</code> and <code>@pondwader/socks5-server</code>, each with its <code>resolved</code> URL on <code>registry.npmjs.org</code> and its sha512 <code>integrity</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The srt package ships no lockfile of its own, so a pin of srt alone would leave its four dependencies to the registry on each install. <code>npm ci</code> installs exactly the tree of the lockfile, refuses a package whose bytes do not match its sha512, and fails on a lockfile whose dependencies do not match those of <code>package.json</code>; it does not compare the override with the lockfile. The override keeps <code>node-forge</code> at 1.4.1: srt asks for <code>^1.4.0</code>, and 1.4.0 has eleven advisories that 1.4.1 fixes.</p><p>The step copies both files into a scratch directory below <code>~/.local/share/research-harness/</code>, beside <code>srt/</code>, and runs <code>npm ci --ignore-scripts --no-audit --no-fund</code> there with the <code>npm</code> of the <code>PATH</code>, so no install script of a package runs. After npm exits 0 and has made <code>node_modules/.bin/srt</code>, the step moves the directory to <code>~/.local/share/research-harness/srt/0.0.81/</code>, links <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> to <code>~/.local/share/research-harness/srt/0.0.81/node_modules/.bin/srt</code>, and removes the directories of other versions and the links below <code>srt/</code>. A file at <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> is replaced by the link. The settings template allows no write at or above <code>~/.local/share/research-harness</code> and denies writes to <code>~/.local/bin</code>, so a Claude Code session can change neither the tree nor the link.</p><p>The step fails, and writes nothing below <code>srt/</code> and nothing in <code>~/.local/bin</code>, when <code>npm</code> is not on the <code>PATH</code>, when <code>node --version</code> is below 22.12.0, the <code>engines.node</code> of srt 0.0.81 (<code>npm ci</code> only warns there), when npm exits non-zero, or when <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> is a directory. For a failed npm, the line holds the cause from npm&#39;s stderr: the text of its <code>npm error code</code> line and its first <code>npm error</code> line of other text, such as <code>code ENOTFOUND; network request to https://registry.npmjs.org/… failed</code>. It removes its scratch directory in every case. A plan finds nothing to do, and has no such step, when <code>srt/0.0.81/package-lock.json</code> holds the bytes of the shipped lockfile, <code>node_modules/.bin/srt</code> exists, and <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> links to it; so a change of the shipped lockfile installs again. The Homebrew copy of srt, where one is installed, stays as it is.</p><h2 id="the-probe-battery" tabindex="-1">The probe battery <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-probe-battery" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The probe battery&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>scripts/probe-agentbox.py</code> checks that each frontend&#39;s settings file holds the boundary that the policy means. Run it after <code>harness setup</code> and <code>harness install --apply</code>, from a plain terminal on macOS: <code>srt</code> refuses a sandbox inside its own, so no session and no <code>agentbox</code> can run it.</p><div class="language-sh vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">sh</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">python3.14</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> ~/Research/Harness/scripts/probe-agentbox.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [--out </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">DIR]</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [--timeout </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">S]</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [--no-model] [FRONTEND ...]</span></span></code></pre></div><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">option</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>FRONTEND</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>claude</code>, <code>opencode</code>, <code>omp</code> or <code>agy</code>; with none named, all four</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--out DIR</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the directory of the report; by default <code>~/Research/.scratch/agentbox-probes/&lt;UTC date and time&gt;</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--timeout S</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the time limit of each frontend probe, in seconds; 300 by default</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>--no-model</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">runs no frontend probe, so the run makes no model call</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The script exits with status 2, runs no probe and prints one line on stderr when <code>SANDBOX_RUNTIME</code> or <code>AGENTBOX</code> is set, on a platform other than macOS, for an argument that is not a frontend or an option, for <code>--out</code> or <code>--timeout</code> with no value, for a <code>--timeout</code> that is not a whole number above 0 in ASCII digits, when <code>~/.local/bin/srt</code> or <code>~/.local/bin/agentbox</code> is not an executable file, and when the settings file of a chosen frontend is missing. The line names the cause, and <code>harness setup</code> or <code>harness install --apply</code> where one of them installs the file.</p><p>It runs four classes of probes for each chosen frontend:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">class</th><th style="text-align:left;">ids</th><th style="text-align:left;">how it runs</th><th style="text-align:left;">what it asks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">listener</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>L:&lt;port&gt;</code>, <code>L:&lt;port&gt;/path</code></td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>curl</code> under <code>srt</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">does each TCP listener on <code>127.0.0.1</code>, on <code>::1</code> or on every interface answer 401, 403 or 407</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">read and write</td><td style="text-align:left;">R1–R8, W1–W14</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>/bin/sh -c</code> under <code>srt</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">can a command read a secret, or write a hook, a launch agent, a shell start file or another frontend&#39;s state</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">network and process</td><td style="text-align:left;">N1–N9, X1–X8</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>/bin/sh -c</code> under <code>srt</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">which hosts it reaches; can it open an app, send an Apple Event, mount, submit a launchd job, signal a process outside or start a sandbox of its own</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">frontend</td><td style="text-align:left;">F1–F7</td><td style="text-align:left;">the frontend under <code>agentbox</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">does it start, do its tools run inside the sandbox, does its own file tool read outside, does it reach Kaimon, and do its own gates hold</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The script lists the listeners with <code>lsof -nP -iTCP -sTCP:LISTEN</code>, outside <code>srt</code>, before the other probes. It asks a listener on every interface (<code>*:&lt;port&gt;</code>) at <code>127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;</code>, and each address and port once, at <code>/</code>. OpenCode&#39;s server takes a password, <code>OPENCODE_SERVER_PASSWORD</code>, and <code>/</code> of it can answer without one, so a listener whose command is <code>opencode</code> is also asked at <code>/path</code>, a route that OpenCode&#39;s binary names, in a row <code>L:&lt;port&gt;/path</code>. A listener answers as the policy means with 401, 403 or 407: 407 is a proxy&#39;s request for authentication, as <code>srt</code>&#39;s own proxies give it. A shell probe runs as <code>~/.local/bin/srt --settings ~/.config/research-harness/sandbox/&lt;policy name&gt;.json -- /bin/sh -c &lt;probe&gt;</code> from <code>~/Research</code>, so its boundary is the settings file alone. A frontend probe runs the frontend&#39;s non-interactive form under <code>agentbox</code>: <code>claude -p</code>, <code>opencode run --auto</code>, <code>omp -p</code> or <code>agy --dangerously-skip-permissions -p</code>, with the prompt as the value of <code>-p</code>; past <code>--timeout</code> it is stopped with its process group. Every probe is harmless where the sandbox fails to stop it: a read prints a count, never content; an append opens the file and writes nothing; a probe that creates a file deletes it. After each frontend&#39;s shell probes, the script removes the launchd job of X4 and unmounts the devfs of X3 where either is left. The probes use a run directory <code>&lt;out&gt;/run</code>, with a <code>secret.txt</code>, a <code>.env</code> and a git repository whose remote is this repository.</p><p>Each probe gets one verdict:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">verdict</th><th style="text-align:left;">meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>match</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the result is the expected one</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>MISMATCH</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the result is not the expected one</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>absent</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the file or directory that the probe names does not exist, so the probe does not run</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>skipped</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the probe does not apply: R8 for <code>agy</code>, X5 with no listener on port 2828 of <code>127.0.0.1</code> or of every interface, the frontend probes with <code>--no-model</code> or with no program on the <code>PATH</code>, F6 with no plain <code>opencode</code> listening</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>timeout</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">the probe ran past its limit</td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;"><code>void</code></td><td style="text-align:left;">F3, which expects a word to be missing, where F1 of the same frontend is not <code>match</code>: a frontend that never started also lacks the word, so the result proves nothing; its detail is <code>F1 shows no start</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">an observation</td><td style="text-align:left;">F5 records the exit status of <code>agy --sandbox</code>, and F7 whether <code>~/.omp/agent/config.yml</code> changed during F1–F4 of <code>omp</code>: <code>unchanged</code>, <code>changed</code>, or <code>absent</code> where the file exists neither before nor after</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The expected value is the policy&#39;s intent, not what the current files do. So a <code>MISMATCH</code> is a finding for the policy, the settings files or the launcher, and a run of a policy with a known gap exits 1. A probe&#39;s output goes into the <code>detail</code> of its row on one line: whole up to 200 characters, else its first and its last 100 characters joined by <code>…</code>, so a failure keeps its cause and its last state. The script writes <code>report.tsv</code>, every row with the columns <code>frontend</code>, <code>id</code>, <code>expected</code>, <code>got</code>, <code>verdict</code> and <code>detail</code>, and <code>report.md</code>: the rows other than <code>L:</code> as a table; a section <code>## Listeners</code> with one line per command, status and verdict of the <code>L:</code> rows, which names the number of ports, the ports in numeric order and the frontends; the count of each verdict over every row in <code>## Counts</code>; and the manual steps: change the model once under <code>agentbox omp</code>, and, where F6 was skipped because no plain <code>opencode</code> listened, run it again with a plain <code>opencode</code> in another terminal. It prints the path of <code>report.md</code> last, and exits 0 when no probe is <code>MISMATCH</code> or <code>timeout</code>, and 1 otherwise; a <code>void</code> alone does not make it 1.</p>`,57)])])}const g=t(a,[["render",n]]);export{f as __pageData,g as default};
